1. Primary Purpose: 
IONM Pipeline — a computational pipeline designed to process and analyze intraoperative neurophysiological monitoring data during surgery.
Primary Purpose — monitor neural signals continuously and identify meaningful changes in neurological function during an operation.
Data Input — receives recorded neurophysiological signals from IONM monitoring channels.
Signal Processing — converts the raw recordings into measurable neural features such as amplitude, latency, frequency, and signal quality.
Baseline Establishment — establishes reference measurements against which later intraoperative measurements can be compared.
Continuous Monitoring — evaluates neural measurements throughout the recording rather than examining only a single point in time.
Change Detection — identifies changes in neural measurements relative to the established baseline.
Multichannel Analysis — compares activity across multiple recording channels to determine whether changes are localized or occur across several channels.
Trend Analysis — tracks neural measurements over time to identify gradual or sudden changes.
Artifact Detection — identifies portions of the recording that may contain electrical, physiological, or other non-neural interference.
Signal Quality Analysis — evaluates whether the recorded data are sufficiently reliable for interpretation.
Evoked Response Analysis — analyzes neural responses produced by controlled stimulation.
Amplitude Analysis — measures changes in the strength of neural responses.
Latency Analysis — measures changes in the timing of neural responses.
Threshold Detection — compares measurements against predefined thresholds to identify potentially significant changes.
Reporting — organizes the processed measurements, detected changes, and trends into a structured analysis that can be reviewed by the user.
Overall Concept — transform continuous raw IONM recordings into structured quantitative information that can be used to monitor neural function throughout surgery.

