3. Statistics:

Signal Amplitude — measures the size or strength of the recorded neural signal.
Peak-to-Peak Amplitude — measures the difference between the highest and lowest points of the signal.
Baseline Amplitude — represents the signal level before the surgical or physiological event being monitored.
Amplitude Change — measures how much the signal changes relative to its baseline.
Percentage Amplitude Change — expresses the amplitude change as a percentage of the baseline.
Latency — measures the time between a stimulus and the resulting neural response.
Response Latency — measures how long it takes for the recorded neural response to occur after stimulation.
Peak Latency — identifies the time at which the response reaches its maximum or minimum amplitude.
Peak Amplitude — identifies the largest measured response following stimulation.
Area Under the Curve (AUC) — measures the total magnitude of a signal over a specified time period.
Root Mean Square (RMS) — measures the overall magnitude of the recorded signal.
Mean — the average value of the recorded signal.
Standard Deviation — measures how much the recorded signal varies around its mean.
Variance — measures the overall spread or variability of the signal.
Minimum — the lowest recorded signal value.
Maximum — the highest recorded signal value.
Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) — measures the strength of the neural signal relative to background noise.
Noise Level — measures unwanted electrical or physiological variation in the recording.
Baseline Noise — measures signal variation present before the relevant stimulation or surgical event.
Frequency — describes how many times a waveform repeats per second.
Spectral Power — measures the amount of signal energy contained within a frequency range.
Dominant Frequency — the frequency containing the greatest amount of spectral power.
Frequency Band Power — measures the signal power within a defined frequency range.
Artifact Level — measures the amount of unwanted interference present in the recording.
Artifact Detection — identifies portions of the recording that may contain non-neural interference.
Channel Quality — describes the quality and reliability of an individual recording channel.
Channel Amplitude — measures the signal magnitude recorded from a specific monitoring channel.
Channel Latency — measures the response timing for a specific monitoring channel.
Baseline Comparison — compares the current neural measurement with the patient's established baseline.
Amplitude Ratio — compares the current amplitude with a reference or baseline amplitude.
Percentage Change From Baseline — expresses the difference between the current measurement and baseline as a percentage.
Trial-to-Trial Variability — measures how much a neural response changes between repeated measurements.
Response Consistency — measures how consistently a neural response occurs across repeated stimulations.
Evoked Response — the measurable neural response produced following an external stimulus.
Evoked Potential — a voltage response generated by neural pathways following stimulation.
Waveform Morphology — describes the shape and structure of the recorded neural waveform.
Peak Detection — identifies important peaks or troughs within the recorded waveform.
Threshold — a predefined measurement level used to identify a meaningful change or event.
Alarm Threshold — a predefined level at which the monitoring system identifies a potentially significant change requiring attention.
Trend — describes how a neural measurement changes over time.
Trend Analysis — evaluates the progression of neural measurements throughout the monitoring period.
Multichannel Comparison — compares measurements across multiple neural recording channels.
Temporal Alignment — aligns neural signals with stimulation events, surgical events, or other time references.
Sampling Rate — the number of measurements recorded per second.
Recording Duration — the total length of time represented by the recording.
